Differential Expression and Functional Dependency Analysis
________________________________________
Step 1: Objective of Task 3
The objective of Task 3 was to functionally validate the prioritized target gene (EGFR) by integrating:
1.	Transcriptomic evidence (TCGA-LUAD)
2.	Functional genomics evidence (DepMap CRISPR)
This step was performed to confirm whether EGFR is biologically active and essential in lung adenocarcinoma.
________________________________________
Step 2: Data Sources Used
Two independent public datasets were used.
1. TCGA Transcriptomics Dataset
Source:
•	UCSC Xena / TCGA Portal
Dataset:
•	TCGA-LUAD RNA-seq (HiSeqV2)
File downloaded:
•	TCGA.LUAD.sampleMap_HiSeqV2
Contents of the file:
•	Rows → genes
•	Columns → patient samples
•	Values → log2 normalized expression values
________________________________________
2. DepMap Functional Genomics Dataset
Source:
•	DepMap (Cancer Dependency Map)
Files downloaded:
•	CRISPRGeneEffect.csv
•	Model.csv
Contents:
•	CRISPRGeneEffect.csv → gene knockout dependency scores
•	Model.csv → cancer type annotation for each cell line
________________________________________
Step 3: Importing Required Python Libraries
The following Python libraries were imported:
•	pandas → data handling
•	numpy → numerical calculations
•	matplotlib → visualization
•	scipy.stats → statistical testing
These libraries were used for data preprocessing, analysis, and interpretation.
________________________________________
Step 4: Loading TCGA Expression Data
The TCGA LUAD expression file was loaded using pandas.
The gene column was renamed to allow identification of individual genes.
This step allowed access to gene-wise expression data.
________________________________________
Step 5: Extraction of EGFR Expression
From the full transcriptomic matrix:
•	Only the row corresponding to the gene EGFR was extracted.
•	The matrix was transposed so that each row represented one patient sample.
This step was performed to analyze EGFR expression across samples.
________________________________________
Step 6: Tumor and Normal Sample Identification
TCGA sample barcodes were used to classify samples:
•	code 01 → tumor
•	code 11 → normal
Each sample was assigned a group label based on barcode suffix.
Samples without valid labels were removed.
________________________________________
Step 7: Creation of Expression Output File
A cleaned output file was generated:
•	File name: EGFR_LUAD_Tumor_vs_Normal.csv
This file contains:
•	sample ID
•	EGFR expression value
•	sample group (tumor or normal)
This file was used for result documentation.
________________________________________
Step 8: Differential Expression Testing
EGFR expression values were separated into:
•	tumor group
•	normal group
A Mann–Whitney U test was applied.
Reason for using this test:
•	RNA-seq expression values are not normally distributed
•	Non-parametric testing provides reliable comparison
The test was used to evaluate statistical difference between tumor and normal samples.
________________________________________
Step 9: Visualization of Expression Pattern
A boxplot was generated to visualize:
•	EGFR expression in tumor samples
•	EGFR expression in normal samples
This helped in visual interpretation of expression differences.
________________________________________
Step 10: Loading DepMap CRISPR Data
The CRISPR gene effect file was loaded using pandas.
The EGFR gene-effect column was extracted from the dataset.
This column represents cell viability loss after EGFR knockout.
________________________________________
Step 11: Integration with Cell Line Metadata
The CRISPR dependency data was merged with the Model.csv file.
Purpose:
•	to identify cancer type for each cell line
•	to filter LUAD-specific cell lines only
________________________________________
Step 12: LUAD Cell Line Filtering
Cell lines with Oncotree code LUAD were retained.
This ensured that functional analysis was specific to lung adenocarcinoma.
________________________________________
Step 13: Dependency Score Interpretation
EGFR gene-effect scores were interpreted using standard thresholds:
•	≤ −0.5 → strong dependency
•	−0.3 to −0.5 → moderate dependency
•	−0.3 → weak or no dependency
These thresholds reflect biological dependency strength.
________________________________________
Step 14: Quantification of Dependency Categories
The number of LUAD cell lines in each dependency category was calculated using NumPy.
The proportion of strongly dependent cell lines was also computed.
This quantified the functional relevance of EGFR.
________________________________________
Step 15: Visualization of Dependency Distribution
A histogram of EGFR dependency scores was generated.
Vertical cutoff lines were added at −0.5 and −0.3 to visualize dependency thresholds.
________________________________________
Step 16: Final Interpretation
Results from both datasets were compared:
•	TCGA → transcriptional behavior
•	DepMap → functional necessity
This integrated analysis helped determine whether EGFR acts as:
•	expression-driven target, or
•	mutation-driven oncogene addiction target.
________________________________________
Final Outcome of Task 3
EGFR showed:
•	limited transcriptional upregulation
•	strong functional dependency in a subset of LUAD cell lines
This supports EGFR as a precision oncology target rather than a universal LUAD target.
